<edge source="us_iran_conflict::energy_supply_shock" target="global_energy_supply_sho::power_supply_capacity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Conflict-driven disruptions to oil and LNG shipments or sanctions on regional producers reduce fuel deliveries to thermal power plants and short-run dispatchable generation, lowering available electricity capacity for industry and data centres.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_conflict::energy_supply_shock" target="russia_ukraine_war::oil_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Disruptions to oil and tanker flows linked to a US‑Iran escalation (e.g., attacks on tankers or Iranian strikes on regional export infrastructure) reduce available crude supply and raise spot and forward benchmarks, directly lifting the oil price level that also underpins markets tied to the Russia‑Ukraine war.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_conflict::energy_supply_shock" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::ai_investment_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">An energy supply shock from US–Iran tensions raises electricity and compute operating costs for data centers and shortens margins, causing firms and investors to delay or downscale AI capex and projects.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
