<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::geopolitical_tension" target="global_energy_supply_sho::oil_supply_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalations in military activity, targeted sanctions, and naval/port interdictions tied to the Russia–Ukraine conflict directly raise the probability of physical interruptions to Russian crude and regional transit routes, increasing oil supply disruption risk.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::geopolitical_tension" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::geo_tension_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalation and geographic spillovers from the Russia–Ukraine war raise allied threat perceptions and military/diplomatic posture, directly increasing bilateral U.S.–China tension through competing alignments, regional deployments, and security signaling.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::geopolitical_tension" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_scrutiny" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Heightened security concerns from the conflict prompt governments to tighten export controls, investment screening and sanctions, directly increasing regulatory scrutiny of cross‑border tech transactions involving U.S. and Chinese firms.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::geopolitical_tension" target="us_iran_conflict::geo_conflict_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.72</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalation and sustainment of the Russia–Ukraine war raises global great‑power friction and diverts Western diplomatic/military focus and resources, creating openings and permissive signalling that enable Iran to escalate actions against US partners, increasing US–Iran conflict intensity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::geopolitical_tension" target="us_political_realignment::regulatory_pressure_on_a" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising geopolitical tensions from the conflict intensify national‑security framing in legislatures and executive agencies, prompting quicker introduction and enforcement of export controls, oversight hearings, and proposed AI restrictions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_political_realignment</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::geopolitical_tension" target="us_political_realignment::ai_compute_capacity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating geopolitics disrupt shipping lanes, raise insurance costs, and constrain component flows (e.g., power supplies, specialty boards), causing delays and shortfalls in deliveries of AI accelerators and datacenter hardware that reduce available compute capacity for affected actors.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_political_realignment</data>
</edge>
